<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shi Jingtang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">892–<a href="/years/942">942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Gaozu of Later Jin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shi Jingtang was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> general and son-in-law of Emperor Mingzong of the Later Tang. In 936 he rebelled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a> and, with the help of the Khitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>, overthrew the Later Tang and founded the Later Jin. In return he ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a> in the north and called himself the son of the Liao emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shi-jingtang.jpg" alt="Shi Jingtang" width="320" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shi_Jingtang_(Jin_Gaozu).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shi Jingtang was born on 30 March 892 to Nieliji, known by the Chinese name Shi Shaoyong, a general under the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-keyong">Li Keyong</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a>. The Old History of the Five Dynasties traced the family to the Han prime minister Shi Fen, apparently to give him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> ancestry; the New History of the Five Dynasties doubted this, and he most likely came from the Shi clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatuo">Shatuo</a> sub-tribe Anqing, of ultimately Sogdian origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-mingzong-of-later-tang">Li Siyuan</a>, then prefect of Dai Prefecture, was impressed by him and gave him his daughter in marriage, and Shi became one of Li Siyuan's two leading officers beside Li Siyuan's adoptive son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-congke">Li Congke</a>, with whom he had a quiet rivalry. When mutineers at Yedu forced Li Siyuan to join them in 926, Shi persuaded him that he could never clear himself with the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-cunxu">Li Cunxu</a> and urged him to march south. After Li Cunxu was killed, Shi helped block Li Jiji's army so that Li Siyuan could take the throne.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-mingzong-of-later-tang">Li Siyuan</a> made him military governor of Baoyi, then deputy commander of the imperial guards, governor of Xuanwu after Zhu Shouyin's revolt in 927, and in 928 defender of Yedu with an honorary chancellor's title. Under Li Conghou the chiefs of staff Zhu Hongzhao and Feng Yun, wary of him, ordered in 934 that he be moved from Hedong to Chengde; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-congke">Li Congke</a> rebelled instead and seized the throne. Shi, frail from illness, was allowed back to Hedong, but by 936 he was widely expected to rebel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>For the help of the Khitan-led Liao dynasty he was called the adopted son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-deguang">Emperor Taizong of Liao</a>, though ten years older. In spring 937 he moved the capital from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> to Daliang to be nearer Fan Yanguang, whom he expected to rebel. Fan rose that summer and drew the general Zhang Congbin into revolt at Luoyang, where Zhang killed two of Shi's sons, Shi Chongxin and Shi Chongyi. Du Chongwei defeated Zhang, and Fan surrendered in the autumn of 938.</p><p>In 940 Li Jinquan of Anyuan rebelled and turned to Southern Tang, but Ma Quanjie defeated its force and peace followed.An Chongrong of Chengde, who saw Shi's submission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> as weakness, called on him in 941 to break with Liao, and Shi went to Yedu to prepare against him, leaving his adopted son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shi-chonggui">Shi Chonggui</a> in charge at Daliang. He died on 28 July 942, and Shi Chonggui succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shi Jingtang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His widow, the Empress Shulü Ping, secured the throne for their second son, Yelü Deguang (Emperor Taizong, r. 927–947), who in 936 helped <b>Shi Jingtang</b> found the Later Jin dynasty in north China and received in return the Sixteen Prefectures around modern Beijing and Datong.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Liao emperor Yelü Deguang enthroned <b>Shi Jingtang</b>, who had rebelled at Taiyuan against the Later Tang, as founding emperor of the Later Jin.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Shi Jingtang</b>, a Shatuo general and son-in-law of Emperor Mingzong of the Later Tang, rebelled at Taiyuan in 936.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-jin-936-947">Later Jin (936-947)</a></li><li><blockquote>The governor <b>Shi Jingtang</b> rebelled at Taiyuan with Khitan help, promising them the Sixteen Prefectures.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-tang">Later Tang</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Ye, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.87,112.54&amp;year=924" aria-label="Taiyuan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7357 802 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Shi Jingtang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7807 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7807 1072h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.87,112.54&amp;year=924">What was here in 924 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
